# Round 94: the C-buffer index arms emitted the TARGET as a bare C operand
# beside a sibling that can REBIND it. The VM pushes container, then index,
# then (for a set) value; these arms did not. Round 88 sequenced
# index-vs-value here and round 93 sequenced target-vs-bound inside
# slice_arg_parts -- neither carried the target axis into these arms, so:
#
#   b[0] is swap of []     (swap rebinds b)   VM 0   AOT 7
#   print of b[(swap of [])]                  VM 5   AOT 0
#
# and when the rebind SHRANK the buffer the AOT fabricated an out-of-range
# death, rc 1, where the VM prints 9. The boxed twin of the same shape was
# already correct (seq_call3 sequences every operand), which is exactly how
# the defect stayed confined to the bt arms. The target now hoists first,
# with a val_incref/val_decref pair -- required, not hygiene: it is a no-op
# only while a buffer-global rebind leaks the old value (#172), and becomes
# the difference between correct and a use-after-free the moment that lands.
b is buffer of 4
b[3] is 9
define swapb() as:
    b is buffer of 2
    return 3
print of b[(swapb of [])]
c is buffer of 4
define swapc() as:
    c is buffer of 2
    return 7
c[0] is swapc of []
print of c[0]
d is buffer of 4
d[0] is 5
define swapd() as:
    d is buffer of 2
    return 0
print of d[(swapd of [])]
e is buffer of 4
e[0] is 5
define swape() as:
    e is buffer of 2
    return 0
s is 0
s is s + e[(swape of [])]
print of s
